            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, and accounts for the majority of your energy use, as shown within the bar graphs located towards the end of this report. I have listed where there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. 

I recommend to outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket, as well as any light switches located on exterior walls. 

I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. As the front door appears to have some warping, it is in reasonable shape, this style of weather stripping will help avoid replacement in regards to energy savings. 

Air leakage was also observed through both of the bathroom exhaust fans. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built directly inside the new unit.

There is also a 'whole house' exhaust fan located at the top of the stairway area, which has been disconnected and no longer functioning. There was a bit of air leakage observed here, which could be solved by romoving the fan, and sealing the hole. 

The attic access hatch has been sealed in the past, but it appears some of the caulking may have dried up and cracked. I recommend to reseal the attic hatch. 

The main source of air leakage was observed to be the header spacing between the first and second floors (mechanical room).  I suspect this may also be the case with the cold floors you experience above the garage. I recommend to have a contractor in, remove the existing extruded polystyrene, and replace this with sprayfoam. Sprayfoam will act as both an insulator, and also as a air barrier.  As your home is quite tall, this generates quite a bit of stack effect.  This may also explain why the first floor is generally cold.  As the warm rises and escapes the home, cold air is brought in from the first floor to 'replace' it.

I have listed another catagory below which refers to the garage ceiling (or 'exposed floor' above the garage).  As I suspect the header space along this portion may also be allowing cold air to infiltrate into the floor, ultimately leading to the floor above the garage section to feel cooler than the rest of the home.  



Savings: For a minimum 20% improvement you could potentially save $ 860 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 26.7 GJ. </airSeal>
                <exposedFloors>

The exposed floor above your garage of your home appear to be 2x8 joists with R28 fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled replacing this with sprayfoam for R30, approximately 5.5 inches. 



Savings:

Although the follow savings appear minimal, this does not include the potential air sealing that would be accomplished from this upgrade. By replacing the current insulation in the floor above the garage, you could potentially save $30 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1.1 GJ. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate your entire exposed floor and a minimum floor area of 14 square metres (150 square feet) must be insulated. You must also increase the insulation value by a minimum of RSI 3.5 (R-20). You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</exposedFloors>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, they are however in reasonable shape, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading the dining room windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Savings: 

By replacing the 9 windows in the home with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $650 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 20.3 GJ.  </windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 121 litre oil-fired hot water tank. If you decide to go with a high-efficiency furnace and when the time comes to replace your hot water heater, I recommend that you replace your hot water heater with an instantaneous oil-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.68 or higher. As the combination of both will allow the verticle flue, as well as the comb air pipe to be removed (and sealed). 



Savings:

By replacing your hot water heater with an instantaneous oil-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.68, you could potentially save $ per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by XX GJ, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

HEATING

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 80.7% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, which also incorporates an ECM fan blower motor. If you choose a high-efficiency oil furnace (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion oil-fired furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, that also incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $1,100 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 36.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



COMBUSTION AIR SUPPLY

A 6 inch combustion air supply pipe was observed to provide combustion air to the boiler and hot water tank.

The combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the furnace or hot water tank is operating.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



BOILER &amp; COMBI HOT WATER

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 80.7% AFUE. As you expressed interest for adding in-floor heating loops, we discussed a potential option of considering a boiler heating system to replace the furnace.  This boiler could also be designed (or built-in the unit) to produce your hot water as well via a secondary heating loop. 



As your home is designed with ducting to be used with a furnace type setup, this would involve installing a fan coil unit, which would allow the boiler heat to be transfered into the existing ducting through an air / liquid heat exchanger. This option would also allow the option to bring in fresh air (as your furnace currently does). 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a direct vented oil-fired boiler with an efficiency of 87% AFUE, you could potentially save $460 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 15.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>DEEP HOME ENERGY RETROFIT PROGRAM

On April 1, 2019, the AEA launched the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (Program), providing up to $20,000 to help owners of older, less efficient homes achieve significant gains in the energy efficiency and home comfort. Completing an exterior wall insulation upgrade is a required first step to participate in this Program, this must be a minimum increase from the current insulation value by R10. In conjunction with an exterior wall insulation upgrade, there are other rebates available for reducing air leakage, upgrading windows and heating systems, and a bonus for reducing total energy use by a minimum 20%.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



If you are interested in adding addition main wall exterior insulation and accessing the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, please do not hesitate to contact me. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house, you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $1.21 per litre 

Propane - $0.68 per litre

Wood - $420 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Michael Goodwin

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: michael.goodwin@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
